## 2. Confound analysis (why this FAIL does not yet cleanly falsify the fork premise)

Two independent confounds sit between the measurements and the conclusion "static-T decoding is not viable on Xe-LP":

**Confound 1 — the graph under test is not the validated graph.** G2's parity PASS belongs to `…_dynamo.onnx`. The G3 numbers belong to `…_dynamo_nchw.onnx`, an improvised rewrite (Contingency B specified stop-and-report; the rewrite went a step beyond). G3.5 proves the rewrite is numerically damaged (corr 0.75 vs the monolith's known small-delta class). A broken graph measured slow is evidence about the rewrite, not the architecture.

**Confound 2 — the 8× self-anomaly.** This same decoder computation runs inside the monolith at warm ~0.9 RTF whole-graph on the same GPU and OV version; the decoder is ~65% of that compute, so decoder-only should land near ~0.6 RTF-equivalent. Instead: ~5. The profile is telling: **2.49 s compile + uniform 11 s infers** is the signature of a compile that selected cheap-to-build reference kernels — the monolith's 17–25 s per-shape JIT was expensive precisely because it built optimized ones. Named suspect: `convolution_gpu_ref__f32`-class fallback (already our documented upstream issue on this model), plausibly triggered by the rewrite's unsqueezed NC1L conv shapes not matching optimized kernel patterns.

Neither confound is resolved by the existing evidence. Both are resolvable cheaply.

## 3. Decision for Nexus (two defensible paths; Architect recommends B)

**A. Full park now.** Gate-honest as written. Spike parks with notes/25–29 + this note as the record; black-box response cache becomes the priority candidate per the standing queue. Cost: the park carries an unexplained 8× anomaly and a broken-graph confound in its conclusion.

**B. One bounded diagnosis-only turn, then park (or fresh gate).** Explicitly *not* a spike extension — no GO path exists without a brand-new gate note. Timebox: one Grok session. Contents:

1. **Per-kernel OV profile** of the NCHW graph (exec-graph / profiling info): name the kernels consuming the 11 s. Predicted branch: ref-class convolutions dominate → Confound 2 root-caused, and the finding folds directly into the existing upstream f32 conv issue draft (same pathology, second graph, stronger filing).
2. **Opset-17 dynamo re-export** — the Contingency B option listed in note_21 §2.1 but never attempted. Predicted branch: convs export in a form OV consumes without the damaging rewrite. If it compiles on GPU.0: re-run parity (bar unchanged) and one timing row. Three outcomes: (i) compiles + parity-clean + fast-class infer → the fork premise revives and earns a **new** gate note; (ii) compiles but still 11 s-class → the slowness is OV's kernel selection for this decoder on Xe-LP regardless of graph form → park hardens to near-conclusive; (iii) fails compile → Contingency B territory again, park stands.
3. No other work. Ears only if a new parity pair is produced.

Recommendation: **B**, because a park with root cause is a stronger park, the diagnosis feeds the upstream filings either way, and the marginal cost is one session against a fork we've already spent the weekend-plus de-risking. If Nexus prefers A, this note is already written to serve as the closing record.

